    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p>Assumptions made are: Constant mortality, Constant fertility, No net migration, Uniform distribution by age within each age group of the female population</p>
<p>We have age intervals of \(15\) years. Thus, let</p>
<p>\(V(1,t)=\) population aged \(0-14\) years last birth day at time \(t\)</p>
<p>\(V(2,t)=\) population aged \(15-29\) years last birth day at time \(t\)</p>
<p>\(V(3,t)=\) population aged \(30-44\) years last birth day at time \(t\)</p>
<p>\(V(4,t)=\) population aged \(45-59\) years last birth day at time \(t\)</p>
<p>\(V(5,t)=\) population aged \(60+\) years last birth day at time \(t\)</p>
<p>\(P_i=\) the probability of surviving from age group \(i\) to the next age group for \(i=1\dots 5\)</p>
<p>\(P_b=\) probability of a baby born surviving to time \(t+15\)</p>
<p>\(f_i^*\) the fertility rate at age group \(i\)</p>
<hr>
<p>First, we arrange a schematic diagram for the population distribution. For \(t=1960\), we have:

<table border="solid" style="width: 100%;">
  <tr>
    <th width="25%" style="text-align:center">Age</th>
    <th width="25%" style="text-align:center">\(1960\)</th>
    <th width="25%" style="text-align:center">\(1975\)</th>
    <th width="25%" style="text-align:center">Projection \(1990\)</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="text-align:center">\(0-14\)</td>
    <td style="text-align:center">\(V(1,t)={@ageset_1a@}\)</td>
    <td style="text-align:center"> \(V(1,t+15)={@ageset_1b@}\) </td>
    <td style="text-align:center">\(V(1,t+30)\)</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="text-align:center">\(15-29\)</td>
    <td style="text-align:center">\(V(2,t)={@ageset_2a@}\)</td>
    <td style="text-align:center">\(V(2,t+15)={@ageset_2b@}\)</td>
    <td style="text-align:center">\(V(2,t+30)\)</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="text-align:center">\(30-44\)</td>
    <td style="text-align:center">\(V(3,t)={@ageset_3a@}\)</td>
    <td style="text-align:center">\(V(3,t+15)={@ageset_3b@}\)</td>
    <td style="text-align:center">\(V(3,t+30)\)</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="text-align:center">\(45-59\)</td>
    <td style="text-align:center">\(V(4,t)={@ageset_4a@}\)</td>
    <td style="text-align:center">\(V(4,t+15)={@ageset_4b@}\)</td>
    <td style="text-align:center">\(V(4,t+30)\)</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="text-align:center">60+</td>
    <td style="text-align:center">\(V(5,t)={@ageset_5a@}\)</td>
    <td style="text-align:center">\(V(5,t+15)={@ageset_5b@}\)</td>
    <td style="text-align:center">\(V(5,t+30)\)</td>
  </tr>
</table>
</p>
<hr>
<p>We start by calculating the survivorship ratios.</p>
<p>After \(15\) years, the surviving members of age group \(V(i,t)\) move to age group \(V(i+1,t+15)\) for \(i=1,2,3\). In mathematical terms: \(V(i+1,t+15) = V(i,t) P_i\). We can use this to compute the survivorship ratios:</p>
<p>\(\text{P}_1=\displaystyle \frac{V(2,t+15)}{V(1,t)}=\displaystyle \frac{{@ageset_2b@}}{{@ageset_1a@}}={@p1@}\) </p>
<p>\(\text{P}_2=\displaystyle \frac{V(3,t+15)}{V(2,t)}=\displaystyle \frac{{@ageset_3b@}}{{@ageset_2a@}}={@p2@}\) </p>
<p>\(\text{P}_3=\displaystyle \frac{V(4,t+15)}{V(3,t)}=\displaystyle \frac{{@ageset_4b@}}{{@ageset_3a@}}={@p3@}\) </p>
<p>For \(i=4\), i.e. the last age group, we have \(V(5,t+15) = V(4,t) P_4 + V(5,t) P_5\). We assume that \(P_5=P_4\), to get:</p>
<p>\(P_4 = P_5 = \displaystyle \frac{V(5,t+15)}{V(4,t) + V(5,t)} \displaystyle \frac{{@ageset_5b@}}{{@ageset_4a@}+{@ageset_5a@}}={@p4@}\) </p>
<hr>
<p>Average number of women in the fertility group \(15-29\) and \(30-44\):</p>
<p>\(w_2=  \displaystyle \frac{V(2,t)+V(2,t+15)}{2} = \displaystyle \frac{V(2,t)+V(1,t)P_1}{2}\)</p>
<p>\(w_3=  \displaystyle \frac{V(3,t)+V(3,t+15)}{2} =  \displaystyle\frac{V(3,t)+V(2,t)P_2}{2}\)</p>
<p>Computing the fertility rates:</p>
<p>\(f_2^*= \displaystyle \frac{ \displaystyle\frac{1}{15}\cdot \text{births from 15-29}}{w_3}=  \displaystyle \frac{ \displaystyle\frac{1}{15}\cdot{@births_1@}}{0.5({@ageset_2a@}+{@ageset_1a@}\cdot {@p1@})}={@f_2@}\)</p>
<p>and</p>
<p>\(f_3^*= \displaystyle \frac{\frac{1}{15}\cdot \text{births from 30-44}}{w_3}=  \displaystyle \frac{ \displaystyle\frac{1}{15}\cdot{@births_2@}}{0.5({@ageset_3a@}+{@ageset_2a@}\cdot {@p2@})}={@f_3@}\)</p>

<p>Within the \(15\) year period \(1960-1975\), we have \({@births_1@}+{@births_2@}\) live births. Of those, \(V(1,t+15)={@ageset_1b@}\) survive until \(1975\), thus we get \(P_b = \displaystyle \frac{{@ageset_1b@}}{{@births_1@}+{@births_2@}} = {@pb@}\)</p>

<p>No. of surviving births \(V(1,t+15)=w_2 \cdot 15 f_2^* \cdot P_b + w_3 \cdot 15 f_3^* \cdot P_b\)</p>
<p>\(\quad \quad =\displaystyle\frac{V(2,t)+V(1,t)P_1}{2} \cdot 15 \cdot f_2^* \cdot P_b + \displaystyle \frac{V(3,t)+V(2,t)P_2}{2} \cdot 15 \cdot f_3^* \cdot P_b\)</p>
<p>\(\quad \quad =\displaystyle V(2,t)\frac{15}{2}\cdot f_2^*\cdot P_b+V(2,t)P_2\cdot \displaystyle\frac{15}{2}\cdot f_3^*P_b+V(3,t)\frac{15}{2}\cdot f_3^*  \cdot P_b+ V(1,t)P_1 \cdot \displaystyle \frac{15}{2} \cdot f_2^* \cdot P_b \)</p>
<p>\(\quad \quad=\displaystyle V(1,t)P_1 \cdot \displaystyle \frac{15}{2}\cdot f_2^*\cdot P_b+V(2,t)(\displaystyle\frac{15}{2}\cdot P_b \cdot P_2 \cdot f_3^* +\frac{15}{2}\cdot P_b \cdot f_2^*)+V(3,t)\cdot \frac{15}{2} \cdot f_3^* \cdot P_b\)</p>
<p>Letting</p>
<p>\(f_1=\displaystyle P_1 \cdot \displaystyle\frac{15}{2} \cdot f_2^* \cdot P_b = {@f1@}\)</p>
<p>\(f_2=\displaystyle P_2 \cdot \displaystyle\frac{15}{2} \cdot f_3^* \cdot P_b+\displaystyle\frac{15}{2}\cdot f_2^* \cdot P_b = {@f2@}\)</p>
<p>\(f_3=\displaystyle \frac{15}{2} \cdot f_3^* \cdot P_b = {@f3@}\),</p>
<p>the above expression becomes \(V(1,t+15)=V(1,t)f_1+V(2,t)f_2+V(3,t)f_3\).</p>
<p>We get the following Leslie matrix:</p>
<p>\(\begin{bmatrix}
V(1,t+15)\\ 
V(2,t+15)\\ 
V(3,t+15)\\ 
V(4,t+15)\\ 
V(5,t+15)
\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}
f_1 & f_2 & f_3 & 0 & 0\\ 
P_1 &0  & 0 & 0 & 0\\ 
 0&P_2  &0  &0  &0 \\ 
 0&0  & P_3 &0  &0 \\ 
 0&  0&  0& P_4 & P_5
\end{bmatrix}~\begin{bmatrix}
V(1,t)\\ 
V(2,t)\\ 
V(3,t)\\ 
V(4,t)\\ 
V(5,t)
\end{bmatrix}\)</p>
<hr>
<p>By evaluating this computation for \(t=1975\), </p>
<p>\(\begin{bmatrix}
V(1,1990)\\ 
V(2,1990)\\ 
V(3,1990)\\ 
V(4,1990)\\ 
V(5,1990)
\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}
{@f1@} & {@f2@} & {@f3@} & 0 & 0\\ 
{@p1@} &0  & 0 & 0 & 0\\ 
 0& {@p2@}  &0  &0  &0 \\ 
 0&0  & {@p3@} &0  &0 \\ 
 0&  0&  0& {@p4@} & {@p5@}
\end{bmatrix}~\begin{bmatrix}
{@ageset_1b@}\\ 
{@ageset_2b@}\\ 
{@ageset_3b@}\\ 
{@ageset_4b@}\\ 
{@ageset_5b@}
\end{bmatrix}\),</p>
<p>we get the following projection:</p>
<table border="solid">
  <tr>
    <th width="15%", style="text-align:center">Age</th>
<th width="15%", style="text-align:center">\(1990\)</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
  <td style="text-align:center">\(0-14\)</td>
    <td style="text-align:center">\({@proj_1@}\)</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="text-align:center">\(15-29\)</td>
    <td style="text-align:center">\({@proj_2@}\)</td>
  </tr>
<tr>
    <td style="text-align:center">\(30-44\)</td>
    <td style="text-align:center">\({@proj_3@}\)</td>
  </tr>
<tr>
    <td style="text-align:center">\(44-59\)</td>
   <td style="text-align:center">\({@proj_4@}\)</td>
  </tr>
<tr>
    <td style="text-align:center">\(60+\)</td>
   <td style="text-align:center">\({@proj_5@}\)</td>
  </tr>
 </table>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
